From d41f8d9ae0087338612ea748a3055a5d7cc8f296 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 19 Jun 2026 21:28:35 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 02935/11362] [outer-loop] prevent the 0.08 shipped-vs-measured
 divergence: SUBMITTED_AGENT_CONFIG + parity gate + canonical-path doc

Q2/Q3 of the operator's questions. Root cause: the eval improved STRONGER opt-in configs
(explorer_bf) while the SUBMITTED default shipped bare BFS, invisible because 'better' was
opt-in and the headline metric was banked-replay levels.

- SUBMITTED_AGENT_CONFIG (arc_competition_agent.py): single source of truth for what ships.
- test_arc_submitted_agent_parity.py (3 tests): asserts the live default == the declared config
  AND the router_wired/world_model_dsl_wired flags reflect REAL imports -> when A1 wires the
  stronger stack, the test FAILS until the shipped config is updated in the same commit. No silent
  divergence possible.
- ops/arc-live-agent-canonical-path.md: disambiguates the 13 overlapping entrypoints -> THE
  submitted path vs dev/eval-only vs deprecated; states the 'strongest config must be the default'
  rule and the cleanup backlog.
- .414 A1 updated to require making the strong config the DEFAULT + updating SUBMITTED_AGENT_CONFIG
  + the parity test passing + reporting the submitted-default solve-rate as the headline.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
